/// known-gap: interp has no overlay classes (`<^>`); the interpreter is being retired
/// An `overlay` line makes a new overlay each time it runs, as `<>` makes a
/// new object: the first run makes the one it names, each later run a kind
/// of its own with no parent, named after its family (`faith_1`). In the
/// code that ran the line, the name means the one just made.
<>Land |
    name: ..
xx
<^>Faith |
    hosts: Land
xx
act found(l, z)
    overlay new_faith <^> Faith
        zeal: z
    xx
    :overlay!(l, new_faith, .2)
    send new_faith
xx
a <> Land | name: "Varn"
b <> Land | name: "Oss"
c <> Land | name: "Tal"
say(found(a, 1))
say(found(b, 2))
say(found(c, 3))
repeat :overlays() as o
    say("{o >> overlay_name} on {o >> host_var}: zeal {o >> zeal}, parents {o >> parents}")
xx
